# File:  start_state_recovery.dpi
# Authors: Tyler Lesthaeghe
# Created: 2026-09-29
# Description:
#   Backs a contact probe off the specimen when the arm has been left in a state
#   the motion planner refuses, so the next run can start without hand jogging.
# Derived From:
#
#########################################################################
# Revision Log
#
# 2026-09-29:  Initial Commit
#
#########################################################################
# Development Notes
#
#
#########################################################################
# Recovering from an arm left touching the part.
#
# THE SITUATION THIS EXISTS FOR. A contact scan ends with the probe on the
# specimen and lifts it off as its last act. Anything that stops the scan
# BETWEEN those two -- an exception, a killed process, a power-cycled
# workstation -- leaves the tip on the part. The planner then refuses every goal,
# because it validates the START state before it plans anything, and a probe
# resting on a modelled specimen is a collision. The refusal arrives at the first
# move of the NEXT run, which is usually a move to a named pose that has nothing
# to do with the part:
#
#     the arm would not move to 'over_table': MoveIt refused it
#
# Nothing in that says the arm is touching something, and the only way out was to
# jog the arm off the part by hand before the pipeline could be used again.
#
# THE ONE MOTION THAT IS SAFE FROM HERE, and the reason this is a recovery rather
# than a workaround: backing the probe straight off along its OWN axis. The tool
# axis is normal to the surface the probe was placed on, so retreating along it
# moves directly away from the part and nothing else on the tooling sweeps
# anywhere new. It cannot reach past the taught envelope, because it only ever
# reduces how far into the work the tool is. A joint move, or a rise in world z
# from a tilted surface, has neither property.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It does not turn collision checking off, and it does not
# change the allowed-collision matrix: /check_state_validity already accepts
# "these links are expected to be touching" and reports every pair that is
# actually in contact, so an undeclared collision somewhere else on the arm still
# reads as a fault and still refuses to move. It also does not retry forever --
# one retreat, then it reports.

# A .dpi is compiled as a function body, so anything the including config needs
# to see has to be declared global before it is assigned.
global start_state_validity
global describe_start_state
global recover_contact_start_state
global START_STATE_RETREAT_M

#: How far to back off, when the caller has no contact settings to say.
#: One approach height is what a pass entry uses, so it is known to be clear of
#: the part and known to be plannable.
START_STATE_RETREAT_M = 0.05


def start_state_validity(contact_links=None, timeout=10.0):
    """Is the arm where it is now a state the planner will plan FROM?

    -> (verdict, why). `verdict` is True, False or None, and None -- the service
    did not answer -- is not the same as False and is not permission to move: an
    unverifiable state is one nobody has checked.

    With `contact_links` empty this is the PLANNER'S OWN VIEW: the service
    already applies the allowed-collision matrix, so what it calls invalid is
    what move_group will refuse to plan from.

    Naming `contact_links` asks a different and weaker question -- "is anything
    OTHER than these touching" -- and the difference between the two answers is
    what identifies a probe left resting on the part.
    """
    node = globals().get("capture_node")
    ros_mod = globals().get("ros")
    if node is None or ros_mod is None:
        return (None, "no capture node and no motion module, so the arm's "
                      "configuration cannot be read")
    q = node.current_joints()
    if q is None:
        return (None, "no /joint_states in the last second, so the arm's "
                      "configuration is unknown -- is the controller running?")
    try:
        verdict = ros_mod.CheckStateValidity(
            list(q), allow_contact_links=list(contact_links or []),
            timeout=timeout)
    except Exception as e:                                       # noqa: BLE001
        return (None, "/check_state_validity raised: %s" % (e,))
    if verdict is True:
        return (True, "the planner accepts the arm's current state")
    if verdict is None:
        return (None, "/check_state_validity did not answer within %.0f s"
                % (timeout,))
    return (False, "the planner reports the arm's current state as in collision")


def describe_start_state():
    """One line on whether the arm can be planned from. Moves nothing."""
    verdict, why = start_state_validity()
    if verdict is True:
        return "  start state: OK -- %s" % (why,)
    if verdict is None:
        return "  start state: UNKNOWN -- %s" % (why,)
    links = list(globals().get("TOOLING_CONTACT_LINKS", []))
    lenient, _ = start_state_validity(contact_links=links)
    if lenient is True:
        return ("  start state: THE PROBE IS ON THE PART -- every goal will be "
                "refused until it is backed off. recover_contact_start_state() "
                "does that along the tool axis; a scan calls it for you.")
    return ("  start state: IN COLLISION, and not at the contact links -- %s. "
            "Something other than the probe is touching, so backing off is not "
            "the fix. Ask the planner what: ros2 service call "
            "/check_state_validity moveit_msgs/srv/GetStateValidity "
            "\"{group_name: ur_manipulator}\"" % (why,))


def recover_contact_start_state(contact=None, positions=None, contact_links=None,
                                retreat_m=None, quiet=False):
    """Back the probe off the part if that is why the planner is refusing.

    -> {"needed", "recovered", "why", "retreat_m"}. `needed` is False when the
    state was already fine, which is the normal case and is why this is cheap
    enough to call before every run.

    `contact` is surface_path_scan.dpi's contact_motion() dict; the retreat is
    flown with its axis speed and acceleration, the same way a pass entry lifts
    off. Without one, START_STATE_RETREAT_M at the same settings.

    ONLY EVER ALONG THE TOOL AXIS, AND ONLY AWAY FROM THE SURFACE -- see the note
    at the top of this file. A state that is invalid for any OTHER reason is
    reported and left exactly as it is: backing off would not fix it, and moving
    the arm on a guess about why the planner is unhappy is the wrong instinct
    next to real hardware.
    """
    links = list(contact_links if contact_links is not None
                 else globals().get("TOOLING_CONTACT_LINKS", []))

    verdict, why = start_state_validity()
    if verdict is True:
        return {"needed": False, "recovered": True, "why": why, "retreat_m": 0.0}
    if verdict is None:
        if not quiet:
            print("  start state could not be checked (%s), so nothing was "
                  "backed off. A move refused from here may be this rather than "
                  "the goal." % (why,))
        return {"needed": None, "recovered": False, "why": why, "retreat_m": 0.0}

    # Invalid. Is it ONLY the links that are allowed to touch the part?
    lenient, lenient_why = start_state_validity(contact_links=links)
    if lenient is not True:
        if not quiet:
            print("  START STATE IN COLLISION, and not only at the links that "
                  "may touch the part (%s)." % (", ".join(links) or "none",))
            print("    Backing off along the tool axis is not the fix: something "
                  "other than the probe is in collision, so nothing was moved. "
                  "Ask the planner what is touching:")
            print("      ros2 service call /check_state_validity "
                  "moveit_msgs/srv/GetStateValidity "
                  "\"{group_name: ur_manipulator}\"")
        return {"needed": True, "recovered": False,
                "why": lenient_why if lenient is False else why,
                "retreat_m": 0.0}

    # The probe is resting on the part and nothing else is touching. This is the
    # recoverable case.
    height = float(retreat_m if retreat_m is not None
                   else (contact["approach_m"] if contact
                         else START_STATE_RETREAT_M))
    if not quiet:
        print("  the probe is resting on the part, which is why the planner is "
              "refusing every goal.")
        print("    backing off %.0f mm along the tool axis -- straight away from "
              "the surface, which is the one move available from here"
              % (height*1e3,))

    lift = globals().get("contact_lift")
    retract = globals().get("contact_retract")
    if lift is None:
        return {"needed": True, "recovered": False,
                "why": "contact_lift() is not available, so the retreat cannot "
                       "be flown; include surface_path_scan.dpi",
                "retreat_m": 0.0}
    settings = contact if contact else _default_contact()
    # With the toolpath in hand, retract knows how far clear the probe already is
    # and lifts only the remainder -- so a second attempt does not walk the arm
    # up and out of reach. Without it, one approach height from wherever it is.
    if positions is not None and retract is not None:
        moved = retract(settings, positions=positions)
    else:
        moved = lift(settings, height)
    if not moved:
        return {"needed": True, "recovered": False,
                "why": "the retreat along the tool axis was itself refused; the "
                       "arm has to be jogged off the part by hand",
                "retreat_m": 0.0}

    verdict, why = start_state_validity()
    if verdict is True:
        if not quiet:
            print("    start state is valid again; the run can continue")
        return {"needed": True, "recovered": True, "why": why,
                "retreat_m": height}
    if not quiet:
        print("    STILL REFUSED after backing off %.0f mm (%s). Do not raise "
              "this and try again -- find out what is touching first."
              % (height*1e3, why))
    return {"needed": True, "recovered": False, "why": why, "retreat_m": height}


def _default_contact():
    """Contact settings for a retreat when the caller passed none.

    Deliberately the near-part speeds: this runs when something has already gone
    wrong, with the tool against the specimen.
    """
    maker = globals().get("contact_motion")
    if maker is not None:
        return maker(near_speed_mps=globals().get("NEAR_PART_SPEED", 0.01),
                     links=list(globals().get("TOOLING_CONTACT_LINKS", [])))
    return {"approach_m": START_STATE_RETREAT_M,
            "near_scaling": 0.05, "axis_scaling": 0.05, "accel": 0.05}
